=== task: mcq medium
title: The empty slot
prompt: A circular queue that stores only `front` and `back` indexes (no size) never lets its last free slot fill up. Why?
* If every slot could fill, a full queue would also have `front == back`, and it couldn't be told apart from an empty one :: Right. Leaving one slot empty means `front == back` always means "empty".
- Python lists can't have every slot filled :: They can; the problem is only with telling full from empty.
- The empty slot is where the next dequeue writes :: Dequeuing reads, it doesn't write.
- It makes the queue faster :: The speed is the same: it costs a slot to keep the two cases apart.
hint: What do `front` and `back` look like when the queue is empty?
hint: And after `back` has gone all the way round?
right: Full and empty both make `front == back`. Stopping one slot short gives "full" its own test, `(back + 1) % cap == front`.
wrong: With every slot usable, filling the queue brings `back` round to `front`, which is also how an empty queue looks. One empty slot keeps the two apart.

=== task: multi medium
title: O(1) in a ring
