=== task: code medium
title: Keep the latest
prompt: Write a class `RecentBuffer(cap)` that keeps the latest `cap` items added to it, in a ring of `cap` slots:
- `add(x)`: add x; when the buffer is full, x overwrites the oldest item,
- `latest()`: return the items as a list, oldest first.

`add` must be O(1).
starter:
```python
class RecentBuffer:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.slots = [None] * cap
        self.cap = cap
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    # your code here
```
solution:
```python
class RecentBuffer:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.slots = [None] * cap
        self.cap = cap
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    def add(self, x):
        if self.size == self.cap:
            self.slots[self.front] = x
            self.front = (self.front + 1) % self.cap
        else:
            self.slots[(self.front + self.size) % self.cap] = x
            self.size += 1

    def latest(self):
        return [self.slots[(self.front + i) % self.cap] for i in range(self.size)]
```
tests:
- (lambda b: [[b.add(x) for x in [21, 22, 25, 24, 23]], b.latest()][1])(RecentBuffer(3))
- (lambda b: [b.add("a"), b.latest()][1])(RecentBuffer(2))
- (lambda b: b.latest())(RecentBuffer(4))
- (lambda b: [[b.add(i) for i in range(10)], b.latest()][1])(RecentBuffer(4))
- (lambda b: [[b.add(i) for i in range(3)], b.latest(), b.front][1:])(RecentBuffer(3))
fail[1]: The buffer keeps the last 3: 25, 24, 23, oldest first.
fail[2]: One item in a buffer of 2 gives a list of that one item. If you got a None too, read only `size` items.
fail[3]: An empty buffer gives an empty list.
fail[4]: After 0 to 9, the last 4 are 6, 7, 8 and 9.
fail[5]: Three items exactly fill the buffer: nothing has been overwritten yet, so front is still 0.
error[IndexError]: Wrap every index with `% self.cap`.
hint: `add`: if the buffer isn't full, write at `(front + size) % cap` and add 1 to size.
hint: If it's full, write over `slots[front]`, then move front on: the next oldest item is now at the front.
hint: `latest` reads `size` items, starting at `front`, with `% cap`.
right: Overwriting the oldest item and moving `front` on keeps the newest `cap` items in O(1) per add, in fixed memory.
wrong: Fill the ring until it's full; after that, overwrite `slots[front]` and move front on. List the items from front, `size` of them.

=== task: code hard
title: Front and back only
prompt: Write a class `RingQueue(cap)` that holds up to `cap` items using only two indexes, `front` and `back` (the next free slot), and an array of `cap + 1` slots, so that one slot is always left empty:
- `is_empty()` is `front == back`, and `is_full()` is `(back + 1) % n == front`, where n is the number of slots,
- `enqueue(x)` returns False if full; otherwise it writes at `back`, moves `back` on, and returns True,
- `dequeue()` returns None if empty; otherwise it reads at `front`, moves `front` on, and returns the item,
- `size()` returns `(back - front) % n`.
starter:
```python
class RingQueue:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.n = cap + 1
        self.slots = [None] * self.n
        self.front = 0
        self.back = 0

    # your code here
```
solution:
```python
class RingQueue:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.n = cap + 1
        self.slots = [None] * self.n
        self.front = 0
        self.back = 0

    def is_empty(self):
        return self.front == self.back

    def is_full(self):
        return (self.back + 1) % self.n == self.front

    def enqueue(self, x):
        if self.is_full():
            return False
        self.slots[self.back] = x
        self.back = (self.back + 1) % self.n
        return True

    def dequeue(self):
        if self.is_empty():
            return None
        x = self.slots[self.front]
        self.front = (self.front + 1) % self.n
        return x

    def size(self):
        return (self.back - self.front) % self.n
```
tests:
- (lambda q: [q.enqueue(1), q.enqueue(2), q.enqueue(3), q.is_full(), q.size()])(RingQueue(2))
- (lambda q: [q.is_empty(), q.dequeue(), q.size()])(RingQueue(3))
- (lambda q: [q.enqueue("a"), q.enqueue("b"), q.dequeue(), q.enqueue("c"), q.enqueue("d"), q.dequeue(), q.dequeue(), q.size()][2:])(RingQueue(2))
- (lambda q: [[q.enqueue(i) for i in range(3)], q.dequeue(), q.dequeue(), q.enqueue(9), q.size(), q.front, q.back][1:])(RingQueue(3))
fail[1]: A queue of capacity 2 has 3 slots. After two items, `(back + 1) % 3 == front`, so the third is refused.
fail[2]: A new queue has `front == back`: empty, size 0.
fail[3]: "c" wraps round, then "d" is refused because the queue is full again; "b" and "c" come out in order.
fail[4]: Two dequeues move front to 2; 9 goes into slot 3 and back wraps round to 0. Size is `(0 - 2) % 4` = 2.
error[IndexError]: Wrap both indexes with `% self.n`.
error[AttributeError]: Define `is_empty`, `is_full`, `enqueue`, `dequeue` and `size`.
hint: `is_empty`: `front == back`. `is_full`: `(back + 1) % n == front`.
hint: `enqueue` writes at `back` then moves it; `dequeue` reads at `front` then moves it; both wrap with `% self.n`.
hint: `size` is `(back - front) % n`: Python's `%` turns a negative difference into the right count.
right: Leaving one slot empty keeps "full" and "empty" apart with only two indexes. `(back - front) % n` counts the items even when `back` has wrapped round behind `front`.
wrong: Use n = cap + 1 slots. Empty when front equals back; full when back is one step behind front. Write at back and read at front, wrapping both; the size is `(back - front) % n`.

=== task: code hard
title: A circular deque
prompt: Write a class `CircularDeque(cap)`: a deque stored in a ring of `cap` slots, with a `front` index and a `size`. Its methods:
- `push_front(x)` and `push_back(x)`: add at either end and return True, or return False if full,
- `pop_front()` and `pop_back()`: remove and return the item at either end, or return None if empty.

The back item is at `(front + size - 1) % cap`. To push at the front, move `front` one step back first: `(front - 1) % cap`, which wraps 0 round to `cap - 1`. Every method must be O(1).
starter:
```python
class CircularDeque:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.slots = [None] * cap
        self.cap = cap
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    # your code here
```
solution:
```python
class CircularDeque:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.slots = [None] * cap
        self.cap = cap
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    def push_front(self, x):
        if self.size == self.cap:
            return False
        self.front = (self.front - 1) % self.cap
        self.slots[self.front] = x
        self.size += 1
        return True

    def push_back(self, x):
        if self.size == self.cap:
            return False
        self.slots[(self.front + self.size) % self.cap] = x
        self.size += 1
        return True

    def pop_front(self):
        if self.size == 0:
            return None
        x = self.slots[self.front]
        self.front = (self.front + 1) % self.cap
        self.size -= 1
        return x

    def pop_back(self):
        if self.size == 0:
            return None
        self.size -= 1
        return self.slots[(self.front + self.size) % self.cap]
```
tests:
- (lambda d: [d.push_back(1), d.push_back(2), d.push_front(0), d.pop_back(), d.pop_front(), d.pop_front(), d.pop_front()][3:])(CircularDeque(3))
- (lambda d: [d.push_front("a"), d.push_front("b"), d.front, d.pop_back(), d.pop_back()][2:])(CircularDeque(4))
- (lambda d: [d.push_back(1), d.push_back(2), d.push_front(3)])(CircularDeque(2))
- (lambda d: [d.pop_back(), d.pop_front()])(CircularDeque(2))
- (lambda d: [d.push_back(5), d.push_front(4), d.push_back(6), d.pop_front(), d.push_front(9), d.slots, d.front][3:])(CircularDeque(3))
fail[1]: The deque is 0, 1, 2: `pop_back` gives 2, then `pop_front` gives 0 and 1, and the empty deque gives None.
fail[2]: Pushing at the front from front 0 wraps round to slot 3, then slot 2. Popping from the back gives "a" (pushed first), then "b".
fail[3]: A deque of capacity 2 is full after two items, so the third push returns False.
fail[4]: Popping an empty deque returns None at either end.
fail[5]: After 4, 5, 6 and one `pop_front`, the front is slot 0; pushing 9 at the front wraps round into slot 2, where the 4 was.
error[IndexError]: Wrap every index with `% self.cap`; `(self.front - 1) % self.cap` is never negative.
error[AttributeError]: Define all four methods.
hint: `push_front`: if full, return False; else set `front = (front - 1) % cap`, write x there, add 1 to size.
hint: `push_back` writes at `(front + size) % cap`. `pop_front` reads at front and moves it on.
hint: `pop_back`: take 1 from size first, then return the item at `(front + size) % cap`.
right: Both ends are reached by index arithmetic, so all four methods are O(1) in fixed memory. Python's `%` makes stepping back from slot 0 land on the last slot.
wrong: Keep front and size. The front item is at `front` and the back item at `(front + size - 1) % cap`. Push at the front by stepping front back with `% cap`; pop at the back by shrinking size.

=== task: code hard
title: A queue that grows
prompt: Write a class `GrowingQueue` that works like a circular queue but never refuses an item: when the array is full, it copies the items, **in queue order**, into a new array twice as big, starting at slot 0, and resets `front` to 0. It starts with one slot. `enqueue` must be O(1) amortized, so grow by doubling. Assume `dequeue` is only called when the queue isn't empty.
starter:
```python
class GrowingQueue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.slots = [None]
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    # your code here
```
solution:
```python
class GrowingQueue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.slots = [None]
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    def enqueue(self, x):
        cap = len(self.slots)
        if self.size == cap:
            new = [None] * (2 * cap)
            for i in range(self.size):
                new[i] = self.slots[(self.front + i) % cap]
            self.slots = new
            self.front = 0
        self.slots[(self.front + self.size) % len(self.slots)] = x
        self.size += 1

    def dequeue(self):
        x = self.slots[self.front]
        self.front = (self.front + 1) % len(self.slots)
        self.size -= 1
        return x
```
tests:
- (lambda q: [q.enqueue(1), q.enqueue(2), q.dequeue(), q.enqueue(3), q.enqueue(4), q.dequeue(), q.dequeue(), q.dequeue()][2:])(GrowingQueue())
- (lambda q: [[q.enqueue(i) for i in range(5)], q.dequeue(), q.dequeue(), q.size][1:])(GrowingQueue())
- (lambda q: [q.enqueue("a"), q.dequeue(), q.enqueue("b"), q.enqueue("c"), q.dequeue(), q.dequeue()][1:])(GrowingQueue())
- (lambda q: [q.size, len(q.slots)])(GrowingQueue())
speed: setup=q = GrowingQueue() | call=[q.enqueue(i) for i in range(n)] | target=O(n)
slow:
```python
class GrowingQueue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.slots = [None]
        self.front = 0
        self.size = 0

    def enqueue(self, x):
        cap = len(self.slots)
        if self.size == cap:
            new = [None] * (cap + 1)
            for i in range(self.size):
                new[i] = self.slots[(self.front + i) % cap]
            self.slots = new
            self.front = 0
        self.slots[(self.front + self.size) % len(self.slots)] = x
        self.size += 1

    def dequeue(self):
        x = self.slots[self.front]
        self.front = (self.front + 1) % len(self.slots)
        self.size -= 1
        return x
```
fail[1]: When 4 arrives, the queue 2, 3 has wrapped round (3 is in slot 0, 2 in slot 1). Copy it in queue order, 2 then 3, or the items come out in the wrong order.
fail[2]: Five items go in, through several growths; the first two out are 0 and 1, and 3 are left.
fail[3]: Items come out in the order they went in, across the growth: a, then b, then c.
fail[4]: A new queue has one slot and no items.
error[IndexError]: After growing, use the new length in `% len(self.slots)`.
hint: In `enqueue`, if `size == len(self.slots)`, make a list twice as long and copy item i of the queue, `self.slots[(front + i) % cap]`, into slot i.
hint: Then set `self.slots` to the new list and `front` to 0, and write x at `(front + size) % len(self.slots)`.
hint: `dequeue` is the usual circular dequeue, using `len(self.slots)` for the wrap.
right: Doubling keeps the copying to fewer than 2n item moves for n enqueues: O(1) amortized, as in the Dynamic arrays lesson. Copying in queue order straightens out a wrapped queue.
wrong: When full, copy the items in queue order into a list twice as long, starting at slot 0, and reset front to 0. Then enqueue as usual.

=== task: code hard
title: A buffer between two programs
prompt: One program puts numbers into a buffer and another gets them out, first in, first out. The buffer is a circular queue with `cap` slots: a put into a full buffer is dropped, and a get from an empty buffer gets nothing. Write `run_buffer(ops, cap)`, where `ops` is a list of operations, each `("put", x)` or `("get",)`, and return a tuple `(got, dropped)`: the list of numbers got, in order, and how many puts were dropped.

For example, `run_buffer([("put", 1), ("put", 2), ("put", 3), ("get",), ("put", 4), ("get",), ("get",)], 2)` returns `([1, 2, 4], 1)`.
starter:
```python
def run_buffer(ops, cap):
    slots = [None] * cap
    front = 0
    size = 0
    got = []
    dropped = 0
    # your code here
    return got, dropped
```
solution:
```python
def run_buffer(ops, cap):
    slots = [None] * cap
    front = 0
    size = 0
    got = []
    dropped = 0
    for op in ops:
        if op[0] == "put":
            if size == cap:
                dropped += 1
            else:
                slots[(front + size) % cap] = op[1]
                size += 1
        elif size > 0:
            got.append(slots[front])
            front = (front + 1) % cap
            size -= 1
    return got, dropped
```
tests:
- run_buffer([("put", 1), ("put", 2), ("put", 3), ("get",), ("put", 4), ("get",), ("get",)], 2)
- run_buffer([("get",), ("put", 5), ("get",), ("get",)], 1)
- run_buffer([], 3)
- run_buffer([("put", 1), ("put", 2), ("put", 3), ("put", 4)], 2)
- run_buffer([("put", 7), ("get",), ("put", 8), ("get",), ("put", 9), ("get",)], 1)
fail[1]: 3 arrives when the buffer is full, so it's dropped. 4 fits after the first get. The gets give 1, 2 and 4.
fail[2]: A get from an empty buffer gets nothing; then 5 is put and got.
fail[4]: Only 2 fit: 3 and 4 are dropped.
fail[5]: A buffer of one slot works if every put is followed by a get.
error[IndexError]: Wrap the indexes with `% cap`, and only get when `size > 0`.
hint: For a put: if `size == cap`, count it as dropped; otherwise write at `(front + size) % cap`.
hint: For a get: if `size > 0`, append `slots[front]` to `got` and move front on.
hint: `op[0]` is "put" or "get", and `op[1]` is the number for a put.
right: Every put and get is O(1), and the buffer never uses more than `cap` slots. Dropping on a full buffer is what real buffers do when the reader can't keep up.
wrong: Run a circular queue: puts go in at `(front + size) % cap` unless it's full (then count a drop); gets take `slots[front]` unless it's empty.

